in a career that has led her to serve as  the head of the un's human rights  monitoring mission in ukraine matilda  bogner has seen more horrors than she  can recall but never anything like this  compared to the other conflicts i've  worked on the intensity  is much higher the numbers of victims  over a short period of time is is much  higher than i have personally seen  in in other conflicts  executions force disappearances  rapes there are few limits to the  violations of international law u.n  monitors are investigating in ukraine  bogner's team of 60 have started looking  into 300 unlawful deaths those are just  the ones that they're aware of from  liberated areas around the capital a  fraction of the overall deaths in  ukraine we can't go to places like  mariupol  we can't go to places where there is  severe hostilities it's just too  dangerous  so i would say that's one of the biggest  constraints when those locations become  accessible bogner says the work of her  team will only expand  already she says nearly every person she  and the team have met have a personal  story of death that requires  documentation  their work to record everyone  is no guarantee of justice  prosecuting war crimes is an onerous  legal undertaking that can take years i  think even just documenting and making  public what is happening is a form of  sort of accountability at least at the  more social social level bearing witness  to horrors of war is painful painstaking  work bogner and her teams share a solemn  sense of duty knowing what they  catalogue here could help how one day  history is remembered and possibly  accounted for  crystal gomanson global news kiev  ukraine
 

